<TopicPage slug="subjects/medicine/expert/health-systems-and-policy">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Health systems are <strong>the machinery between medical knowledge and actual health</strong>:
    the financing rules, workforces, hospitals, supply chains and information systems that decide
    whether a treatment proven in a laboratory reaches a patient who needs it. A brilliant drug
    in a broken system reaches no one. Comparative health policy is therefore not ideology — it
    is a natural experiment running continuously across roughly 200 countries, with the same
    diseases entering different machinery and measurable outcomes coming out. The cleanest way to
    see the system whole is to follow the organ systems it must serve: a blood service, a cardiac
    network, an immunisation programme and a pandemic stockpile are each a policy answer to a
    specific anatomical failure mode. This chapter walks that correspondence — anatomy on one
    side, policy machinery on the other.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the models, the trade-off triangle, and the leverage points" widget="equationStep" config={sysCfg} />

  <h2>Blood: the perishable supply chain</h2>
  <p>
    Blood is the health system's most perishable commodity, and its supply chain shows why
    logistics is clinical policy. Collected blood is tested, then separated into components — red
    cells, platelets, plasma — each with its own clock: red cells survive refrigeration for
    around five to six weeks, platelets only about a week, and plasma must be frozen to last
    months. That shelf life means the blood supply cannot be stockpiled or manufactured; it must
    be continuously replaced by donors. The system rests on a scientific discovery and a policy
    choice. Karl Landsteiner's identification of the ABO groups in 1901 (Nobel Prize, 1930)
    turned transfusion from a lethal lottery into routine surgery; the policy choice is that most
    rich countries draw on voluntary, unpaid donors, after evidence that paid donor systems
    attracted higher-risk blood. The system's weaknesses are also demographic: patients with
    sickle cell disorders — most born in sub-Saharan Africa — need closely matched blood
    (particularly the Ro subtype), which is why blood services actively recruit donors of Black
    African and Caribbean heritage. A health system is only as strong as this week's donation
    queue.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "blood is just another product the market can supply." It
    cannot be synthesised at scale, it expires faster than most groceries, and the evidence base
    for safe supply depends on altruistic donation — which is exactly why every serious health
    ministry treats blood as strategic infrastructure, not merchandise.
  </p>

  <h2>The heart: financing the fight against the biggest killer</h2>
  <p>
    Cardiovascular disease kills an estimated 17.9 million people a year — about a third of all
    deaths worldwide — so health-system design is, more than anything, cardiac-care design. The
    clinical logic: a heart attack (myocardial infarction) kills heart muscle by the minute, and
    the definitive treatment, angioplasty to reopen the blocked artery, works best fast. Systems
    therefore organise themselves around the clock, not the hospital. Guidelines demand
    <em>door-to-balloon</em> times of roughly 90 minutes; London's response in the 2000s became
    the textbook case — ambulance crews were retrained to diagnose the ECG in the field and drive
    straight past the nearest emergency department to a 24/7 specialist hub, consolidating eight
    receiving centres into fewer, busier ones. Survival improved: the volume–outcome literature
    consistently shows hospitals performing more procedures per year lose fewer patients. The
    financing layer follows the same logic — rich systems pay for primary-care statins and blood
    pressure control precisely because one prevented infarction funds a great deal of prevention.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "more hospitals nearby must mean better care." For
    time-critical conditions the opposite is often true: a network of a few high-volume
    specialist centres, reached by protocols that bypass smaller units, outperforms a map dotted
    with low-volume general ones. Proximity to a building is not proximity to competence.
  </p>

  <h2>Vessels and circulation: the stroke clock</h2>
  <p>
    Circulatory policy is mostly about one non-renewable resource: neurons. When a stroke cuts
    blood flow to the brain, roughly 1.9 million neurons die each minute (Saver's often-cited
    2006 calculation) — the origin of the slogan <em>time is brain</em>. The system's response is
    a carefully tiered pipeline: rapid recognition (the FAST public-awareness test), CT imaging
    to distinguish ischaemic clot from haemorrhage, clot-dissolving drugs within about 4.5 hours,
    and mechanical thrombectomy — physically pulling the clot out — now extended to 24 hours in
    selected patients whose imaging shows salvageable tissue. That extension is a clean worked
    example of evidence changing policy: the DAWN trial (2018) found that patients treated six to
    twenty-four hours after onset reached functional independence about half the time, against
    roughly 13% on medical management alone — results so strong the trial stopped early. Upstream,
    the same vessels set the system's biggest prevention task: about 1.28 billion adults aged
    30–79 live with hypertension, and close to half are unaware of it (WHO, 2023) — a mass of
    silent risk that only primary care, screening and cheap generic drugs can reach.
  </p>

  <h2>Immunity: the vaccination contract</h2>
  <p>
    The lymphatic and immune system is the one organ system where the health system's core
    product is not treatment but a population-level bargain. Immunisation creates individual
    memory; at scale, immune people block transmission, protecting even the unvaccinated — herd
    immunity. The threshold is arithmetic, not aspiration: it is 1 − 1/R₀, where R₀ is how many
    people one case infects in a fully susceptible population. Measles, with R₀ of 12–18, needs
    roughly 92–95% immune — far above what natural infection ever achieved without catastrophic
    cost, and only attainable because the two-dose MMR vaccine protects about 97% of recipients.
    The bargain is fragile in both directions. Smallpox was declared eradicated in 1980 because
    vaccination plus surveillance closed the gap everywhere; when UK measles coverage slipped a
    few points after the fraudulent 1998 Wakefield paper, the country lost its WHO measles-free
    status within two decades of regaining it. Immunisation policy is therefore the study of how
    trust, logistics and arithmetic interlock — drop the coverage, and the threshold is breached
    in a season.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "herd immunity means the disease is finished once the
    threshold is passed." The threshold is a probabilistic shield, not a wall: newborns, the
    immunocompromised and the unlucky still get infected, and imported cases spark local
    outbreaks whenever local coverage dips. Nor is "letting everyone catch it" a legitimate
    route to the threshold — the deaths are paid up front.
  </p>

  <h2>The respiratory system: pandemics as stress tests</h2>
  <p>
    The lungs connect every human being to every other — which is why respiratory disease
    defines the system's worst days. Tuberculosis still kills around 1.25 million people a year
    (WHO, 2023) despite a cure existing since the 1940s–50s: the machinery between knowledge and
    health — diagnosis, drug supply, adherence support — is the failure, not the science. Chronic
    obstructive pulmonary disease ranks among the world's top causes of death. And COVID-19 was
    the century's stress test: the same virus produced wildly different death rates across
    comparable countries, and the WHO estimated roughly 15 million excess deaths in 2020–2021 —
    a direct readout of system quality. The success story inside the failure: the SARS-CoV-2
    genome was published on 11 January 2020, and the first mRNA vaccine dose entered a human arm
    just 63 days later — a platform built for exactly this, while authorisation still awaited
    full-scale trials with tens of thousands of volunteers. Preparedness policy (stockpiles,
    sequencing capacity, ventilator surge plans) is the respiratory system's infrastructure,
    bought in peacetime precisely because it cannot be bought in time.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the COVID vaccines were developed too fast to be safe." The
    speed came from decades of prior platform research, unlimited funding and manufacturing
    started at financial risk before approval — not from skipped trials. Authorisation waited for
    phase 3 data; the compression was in paperwork queues and parallel production, not in the
    evidence.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The models, named.</strong> The widget's four financing models deserve their
    mechanisms: <em>Beveridge</em> systems (Britain's NHS, 1948) fund care from general
    taxation, free at use, rationed by waiting; <em>Bismarck</em> systems (Germany, from the
    1880s) run on compulsory social-insurance funds negotiated with providers, rationed by
    capacity and regulation; national health insurance (Canada) is single-payer, public
    financing with private delivery; and out-of-pocket systems (much of the developing world)
    ration by price — which is the polite way of saying by poverty. Each model is a different
    answer to the same triangle, and the Commonwealth Fund's comparative scoreboards show the
    design details (primary-care strength, negotiated prices, low administrative complexity)
    predict performance better than the label.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Primary care is the high-leverage layer.</strong> Barbara Starfield's cross-national
    research programme established the correlation the widget names: health systems with strong
    primary care — gatekeeping, continuity, first-contact prevention — achieve better outcomes
    at lower total cost than specialist-heavy systems. The mechanism is arithmetic as much as
    medicine: most illness is minor, chronic or preventable, and a strong first layer handles
    it where it is cheapest, reserving hospitals for what hospitals are for. Systems that
    skimp primary care pay the difference in emergency departments — the most expensive real
    estate in medicine, working as the safety net for the gatekeeping that never happened.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The trade-off triangle, formalised.</strong> Every system chooses a position among
    access, quality and cost, and health economists make the choice explicit with the QALY — a
    quality-adjusted life year, one year of perfect health. Britain's NICE has historically
    treated roughly £20,000–30,000 per QALY as the flag at which a treatment must justify itself;
    a drug costing £100,000 per QALY is not a treatment the system can buy for everyone. The
    arithmetic is uncomfortable and honest: budgets are finite, so a system that says yes to
    everything is saying no to somebody else by another route.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The workforce is the binding constraint.</strong> Machines and molecules matter less
    than people: the WHO projects a shortfall of around 10 million health workers by 2030,
    concentrated in the countries with the highest disease burden. Health-worker migration from
    poor to rich systems is the quiet transfer this creates — which is why the WHO adopted a
    voluntary code of practice on ethical recruitment (2010). A health system is, in the end, an
    employment scheme for skilled people; training pipelines have 10–15 year lags, so today's
    shortages were decided a decade ago.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Why the US bill is so large.</strong> The US spends near 17% of GDP on health —
    roughly double, in share terms, most peers — with middling life expectancy. The careful
    comparative work (Anderson, Reinhardt, Papanicolas & Jha) attributes the gap mainly to
    prices and administrative overhead, not to greater utilisation: Americans do not visit
    doctors more, they pay more per visit, per drug and per scan, and multi-payer billing
    consumes several times the administrative share of single-payer peers. The lesson for any
    system: the label (Beveridge tax-funded, Bismarck insurance, single-payer, mixed) predicts
    less than the design details — primary-care strength, negotiated prices, standardised
    administration.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Prevention still underfunds itself: its payoffs arrive
    years later, to different budgets, so the payer who saves is not the payer who spends — an
    institutional mismatch no country has fully solved. Universal health coverage (SDG 3.8)
    remains unfinished for billions. And the WHO's Pandemic Agreement, adopted in 2025, is the
    newest attempt to fix COVID's coordination failures — pathogen-sharing, vaccine equity,
    surge supply — into a treaty that outlives the news cycle.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> WHO Global Health Expenditure Database; Commonwealth Fund international comparisons; Starfield, Primary Care; Papanicolas & Jha, JAMA (health spending analyses).</p>
